is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the OSPF adjacency on the core backhaul between router_core_1 and router_core_3. The failure is at Layer 1 on router_core_1’s eth1 interface, which carries the point-to-point /31 link to router_core_3 eth0. On router_core_1, ethtool shows Link detected: no on eth1 and ip -br confirms eth1 is DOWN with 172.16.0.2/31. OSPF neighbors on router_core_1 list only the peers on eth0 and eth2; there is no neighbor on eth1, confirming the adjacency is down on that side. On router_core_3, eth0 is UP/LOWER_UP with 172.16.0.3/31, but a direct ping sourced from eth0 to 172.16.0.2 returns Destination Host Unreachable, demonstrating the far end is not present even though the local interface remains electrically up. Mechanistically, loss of carrier on router_core_1 eth1 stops OSPF hellos, so the adjacency to router_core_3 drops. Traffic remains unaffected because OSPF has converged to the alternate path via router_core_2 (r1–r2–r3), preserving reachability to the server farm but eliminating redundancy on pod 1’s path. Root cause: physical link/cable or port failure on router_core_1 eth1 for the r1:eth1 ↔ r3:eth0 interconnect. Corrective fix: dispatch to repair/replace the patch or optics on router_core_1 eth1 (and reseat/check the port), then bring the interface back; OSPF will reestablish automatically once carrier returns. === END ===